DSP.mod(6,{"m6-lifecycle":{"id":"m6-lifecycle","slug":"the-experiment-lifecycle","m":6,"title":"The experiment lifecycle","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m5-hypothesis"],"tags":["ab-testing","experimentation","product"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The end-to-end lifecycle of an A/B test: hypothesis, metrics, randomisation unit, sizing, running, SRM check, analysis and decision.","keyTerms":["hypothesis","primary metric","guardrail metric","randomization unit","sample ratio mismatch","duration","decision rule"],"interviewQs":["Walk me through designing an A/B test for a checkout redesign.","What is a guardrail metric?","What is sample ratio mismatch and what do you do about it?","What should you randomise on?"],"mistakes":["Choosing several primary metrics","Randomising by session but analysing by user","Ending the test as soon as it looks significant"],"practice":"Write a one-page design for an A/B test of a new delivery-fee message, covering all seven steps.","related":["m6-sample-size","m6-pitfalls","m13-feature-success"],"points":"1. Define the goal and hypothesis: \"Changing the checkout button color will increase conversion.\"\n2. Choose metrics\n- Primary (success) metric: one, sensitive, tied to the goal (e.g., checkout conversion rate).\n- Secondary metrics: diagnostic (add-to-cart rate, AOV).\n- Guardrail metrics: must not degrade (latency, crash rate, refund rate, unsubscribe rate).\n3. Choose the randomization unit: user (most common), session, device, cookie, store/city (for marketplaces, use geo or switchback to avoid interference).\n4. Estimate sample size (see below) and duration (cover at least 1-2 full weekly cycles; avoid holidays/sales skew).\n5. Run: check for Sample Ratio Mismatch (SRM) with a chi-square test on group sizes (if you planned 50/50 and got 50.6/49.4 with large n, something is broken).\n6. Analyze: pre-planned test, CI for the difference, segment cuts only as hypothesis generators.\n7. Decide: ship / iterate / kill, considering effect size, cost, guardrails, novelty."},"intuitive":"<p>An A/B test is a fair race between two versions. You flip a coin for each visitor to decide whether they see the old page (A) or the new page (B), wait until enough people have visited, and compare one agreed measure (like &quot;did they buy?&quot;). The coin flip is what makes it fair: the two groups are alike in every way except the page, so any difference can be blamed on the page. Before you start you decide what counts as winning, how many people you need, and what must not get worse (like page speed or refunds).</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Near-certain in product-company interviews: &quot;design an A/B test for X&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Hypothesis, metrics, unit, sample size, run, analyse, decide, with the decision rule written before you start.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;An A/B test is a controlled experiment. The lifecycle has seven steps. One: state the hypothesis and the decision it feeds. Two: pick a primary metric, a few guardrail metrics that must not get worse, and the unit of randomisation, usually the user. Three: compute the sample size and run time from the baseline, the minimum effect worth detecting, alpha and power. Four: randomise and check the setup with an A/A test or a sample ratio mismatch check. Five: run for the full planned duration, including whole weeks. Six: analyse with the pre-planned test. Seven: decide, document and monitor after launch. The catch: peeking at results early and stopping on the first significant day inflates false positives.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Write the one-page plan before any user sees the change","html":"<p>A food-delivery app wants to lower the free-delivery threshold from 299 to 249 rupees.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hypothesis and decision.</strong> &quot;A lower threshold raises orders per user enough to pay for the extra free deliveries. If it does, we roll it out in all cities.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Primary metric.</strong> Contribution margin per user over 14 days. Orders alone would reward giving away delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>Guardrails.</strong> Average order value (it may fall as people stop adding items to reach 299), delivery-partner wait time, and cancellation rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Unit.</strong> Randomise by user, not by order, so the same person always sees the same threshold.</li>\n<li><strong>Size and duration.</strong> Work out the sample size from the baseline and the smallest worthwhile lift (topic 6.2), then round the duration up to whole weeks, say 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Checks during the test.</strong> Sample ratio mismatch on day 1; no decisions from peeking.</li>\n<li><strong>Decision rule, written now.</strong> Ship if margin per user rises with the confidence interval above zero and the guardrails are flat; iterate if orders rise but margin falls; stop otherwise.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Everything is decided before the data exists, which is what stops you from finding a convenient story afterwards.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-seven-steps-with-a-running-example","text":"The seven steps, with a running example","level":3},{"id":"h-sample-ratio-mismatch-srm","text":"Sample ratio mismatch (SRM)","level":3},{"id":"h-a-a-tests","text":"A/A tests","level":3},{"id":"h-the-decision-rule-written-in-advance","text":"The decision rule, written in advance","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Define the goal and hypothesis:</strong> &quot;Changing the checkout button color will increase conversion.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Choose metrics</strong><ul>\n<li><strong>Primary (success) metric:</strong> one, sensitive, tied to the goal (e.g., checkout conversion rate).</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary metrics:</strong> diagnostic (add-to-cart rate, AOV).</li>\n<li><strong>Guardrail metrics:</strong> must not degrade (latency, crash rate, refund rate, unsubscribe rate).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the randomization unit:</strong> user (most common), session, device, cookie, store/city (for marketplaces, use geo or switchback to avoid interference).</li>\n<li><strong>Estimate sample size</strong> (see below) and <strong>duration</strong> (cover at least 1-2 full weekly cycles; avoid holidays/sales skew).</li>\n<li><strong>Run</strong>: check for <strong>Sample Ratio Mismatch (SRM)</strong> with a chi-square test on group sizes (if you planned 50/50 and got 50.6/49.4 with large n, something is broken).</li>\n<li><strong>Analyze:</strong> pre-planned test, CI for the difference, segment cuts only as hypothesis generators.</li>\n<li><strong>Decide:</strong> ship / iterate / kill, considering effect size, cost, guardrails, novelty.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-seven-steps-with-a-running-example\">The seven steps, with a running example</h3>\n<p>Scenario: Flipkart-style checkout page; the team wants to change the &quot;Place order&quot; button and shorten the form.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Goal and hypothesis.</strong> &quot;Shortening the checkout form will raise checkout conversion.&quot; A good hypothesis names the change, the metric and the expected direction.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Metrics.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Role</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Primary (success)</strong></td><td>The one decision metric; sensitive, close to the change</td><td>checkout conversion rate (orders / checkout starts)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Secondary (diagnostic)</strong></td><td>Explain <em>why</em> it moved</td><td>add-to-cart rate, time to complete, average order value</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Guardrail</strong></td><td>Must not get worse</td><td>page latency, payment failure rate, refund rate, support contacts, cancellations</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Pick <strong>one</strong> primary metric in advance. More primaries means more chances for a false win.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Unit of randomisation.</strong> Usually the <strong>user</strong> (logged-in ID) so a person always sees the same version. Alternatives: session (leaks across visits), device/cookie (cross-device users counted twice), or <strong>cluster/geo</strong> (city, store) when users interact, such as marketplaces (see interference in the pitfalls topic). The unit of analysis should match the unit of randomisation.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Sample size and duration.</strong> Compute the sample size from baseline rate, minimum detectable effect (MDE), α and power (next topic). Run for <strong>at least one or two full weekly cycles</strong> to average over weekday/weekend behaviour, and avoid sales days and festivals that distort behaviour.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Run and monitor health.</strong> Do not look at outcomes to decide early; do check <strong>data quality</strong>: is traffic splitting as designed? (SRM below), are events logging, are guardrails breached catastrophically (kill switch)?</p>\n<p><strong>6. Analyse.</strong> The pre-planned test, a confidence interval for the difference, and effect on guardrails. Treat segment cuts as hypothesis generators, not conclusions.</p>\n<p><strong>7. Decide.</strong> Ship, iterate or kill, considering effect size (is +0.3% worth the complexity?), cost, guardrails and risks such as novelty effects.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-sample-ratio-mismatch-srm\">Sample ratio mismatch (SRM)</h3>\n<p>If you planned a 50/50 split and the observed counts deviate more than chance allows, something is broken: assignment bugs, bot filtering applied to one arm, a redirect that loses users, a logging failure in one variant. Test the counts with a chi-square goodness-of-fit test against the planned ratio; flag at a strict threshold (p &lt; 0.001).</p>\n<p>Check your own counts:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"srm","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\nstats.chisquare([<span class=\"hljs-number\">257_500</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">242_500</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">250_000</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">250_000</span>]).pvalue    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 51.5 / 48.5 at n = 500k  -&gt;  ~1e-100: SRM, invalidate</span>\nstats.chisquare([<span class=\"hljs-number\">50_300</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">49_700</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">50_000</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">50_000</span>]).pvalue        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 50.3 / 49.7 at n = 100k  -&gt;  0.058: within chance</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>A 51.5/48.5 split looks small, but at half a million users it is about 21 standard deviations away from 50/50, so it is certainly a bug. <strong>Never analyse an experiment that has SRM</strong>: the two groups are no longer comparable. Find the cause, fix it, and rerun.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-a-tests\">A/A tests</h3>\n<p>Run the same experience in both arms to check the platform: about 5% of A/A tests should show p &lt; 0.05 and the p-value distribution should be uniform. Failures reveal broken randomisation, inflated variance (wrong unit of analysis) or logging bias.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-decision-rule-written-in-advance\">The decision rule, written in advance</h3>\n<p>&quot;Ship if conversion improves by at least +0.5 percentage points with the 95% CI above 0 and no guardrail degrades by more than its tolerance. Otherwise iterate (if the CI includes meaningful gains) or kill.&quot; Writing it before the data arrives prevents post-hoc rationalisation.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m6-lifecycle~1","q":"After two weeks the test shows a statistically significant +2% conversion, but the page load time guardrail got 300 ms slower. Do you ship?","a":"<p>Guardrails exist precisely for this situation. Check whether the slowdown exceeds the pre-agreed tolerance; estimate the downstream cost (latency usually hurts conversion, retention and SEO over time); look for a cause (heavy new component) and whether it can be fixed without losing the gain; and consider a follow-up test with the optimised build. If the tolerance is breached the default is <strong>do not ship as is</strong>; ship only with explicit sign-off that the gain outweighs the cost.</p>\n","s":"Not automatically: quantify the trade-off, investigate the latency, and weigh it with the pre-agreed guardrail tolerance."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-lifecycle~ca0_1","q":"Why run an experiment for full weeks even if significance appears on day 3?","a":"<p>Behaviour varies by weekday and early results are noisy; stopping at the first significant peek inflates the false-positive rate.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-lifecycle~ca0_2","q":"What is a guardrail metric? Give an example.","a":"<p>A metric that must not degrade while we optimise the primary one, such as page load time or unsubscribe rate when testing a new notification.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-lifecycle~ca0_3","q":"Expected 50/50 split but you see 52/48 on a million users. What does it signal?","a":"<p>A sample ratio mismatch: a chi-square test will flag it as impossible by chance, meaning a bug in assignment or logging; do not trust the results.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m6-lifecycle~ta1_1","q":"<p>A team tests a new daily push notification and plans to judge it on &quot;app opens per user&quot;. Name the main risk with that metric and three guardrails you would add.</p>\n","a":"<p>Notifications almost always increase app opens in the short term, so the primary metric is easy to move without creating value, and opens are a weak proxy for value. Guardrails: notification opt-out or uninstall rate, orders or revenue per user (does value follow the opens?), and longer-term retention, for example 28-day active users. A good primary metric would be orders per user or retained users, with opens as a secondary metric.</p>\n"}]},"m6-sample-size":{"id":"m6-sample-size","slug":"sample-size-the-back-of-envelope-method","m":6,"title":"Sample size: the back-of-envelope method","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m6-lifecycle"],"tags":["ab-testing","experimentation","power"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"How to compute the sample size and duration of an A/B test with the 16 sigma squared over delta squared rule, the exact statsmodels version, and ways to reduce the required sample.","keyTerms":["minimum detectable effect","power","alpha","sample size formula","baseline conversion","variance","test duration"],"interviewQs":["Estimate the sample size per group to detect a 1 percentage point lift on a 10% conversion rate.","What affects the required sample size?","How long must we run the test?"],"mistakes":["Using relative lift as if it were absolute","Forgetting that the formula is per group","Planning a test that cannot reach the required sample in a sensible time"],"practice":"Compute the sample size for three MDEs at a 10% baseline and convert each into days of traffic.","related":["m6-lifecycle","m5-hypothesis","m6-advanced"],"points":"For a two-sided test with alpha = 0.05 and power = 0.8, per-group sample size:\n\n- Continuous metric: n ~ 16 * sigma^2 / delta^2\n- Proportion: n ~ 16 * p(1 - p) / delta^2\n\nwhere delta is the minimum detectable effect (MDE) in absolute terms.\n\nExample: baseline conversion 10%, want to detect +1 pp (10% to 11%).\nn ~ 16 x 0.1 x 0.9 / 0.01^2 = 16 x 0.09 / 0.0001 = 14,400 per group.\n\nLevers to reduce required sample size: larger MDE, lower-variance metric, CUPED/variance reduction, one-tailed (rarely justified), accept lower power."},"intuitive":"<p>To notice a small difference between two groups you need lots of people, like needing a very precise scale to tell whether two apples differ by one gram. Smaller differences need more people; noisier measurements need more people; wanting to be more certain needs more people. There is a handy rule of thumb: people needed in each group is about 16 × (how noisy the measure is) ÷ (the smallest difference you care about) squared. Halve the difference you care about and you need four times as many people.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers ask you to do this on the spot, in your head.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> n per group ≈ 16σ²/δ²; for a proportion, 16p(1−p)/δ². Halving δ quadruples n.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Sample size depends on four things: the baseline rate or variance, the smallest effect I care about, the significance level and the power. The back-of-envelope formula for each group is about sixteen times variance divided by delta squared, for five per cent alpha and eighty per cent power. For a conversion of ten per cent and a target lift of one percentage point, variance is point one times point nine, about 0.09, so n is sixteen times 0.09 over 0.0001, roughly fourteen thousand per group. Halving the detectable effect quadruples the sample. The catch: I divide the total by daily traffic to get run time, and a smaller effect than planned means an underpowered test.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Size a test with the rule of thumb","html":"<p>Baseline conversion is 8%. The team cares about a lift of 1 percentage point (to 9%). There are 3,000 eligible users a day.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Variance of a yes/no outcome:</strong> p(1 − p) = 0.08 × 0.92 = 0.0736.</li>\n<li><strong>The rule of thumb</strong> for 5% significance and 80% power: n per group ≈ 16 × variance / δ², where δ is the difference to detect.</li>\n<li><strong>Plug in:</strong> 16 × 0.0736 / 0.01² = 16 × 0.0736 / 0.0001 = <strong>11,776 users per group</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Total and duration:</strong> 2 × 11,776 = 23,552 users ÷ 3,000 a day ≈ 7.9 days. Round up to <strong>2 full weeks</strong> so that every weekday is covered at least once and novelty has time to fade.</li>\n<li><strong>What if the team wants to detect 0.5 points?</strong> δ halves, so n quadruples: 47,104 per group, about a month of traffic. Is a 0.5-point lift worth a month? That is a business question.</li>\n<li><strong>If the traffic is not there,</strong> raise the minimum detectable effect, use a more sensitive metric, reduce variance with CUPED, or accept a longer run.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Sample size follows from four inputs: baseline, minimum effect, significance and power. Halving the effect you want to detect costs four times the users.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-where-the-rule-comes-from","text":"Where the rule comes from","level":3},{"id":"h-worked-examples","text":"Worked examples","level":3},{"id":"h-from-sample-size-to-duration","text":"From sample size to duration","level":3},{"id":"h-levers-to-reduce-the-required-sample","text":"Levers to reduce the required sample","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>For a two-sided test with alpha = 0.05 and power = 0.8, per-group sample size:</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Continuous metric:</strong> n ~ 16 * sigma^2 / delta^2</li>\n<li><strong>Proportion:</strong> n ~ 16 * p(1 - p) / delta^2</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>where delta is the minimum detectable effect (MDE) in absolute terms.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Example:</strong> baseline conversion 10%, want to detect +1 pp (10% to 11%).\nn ~ 16 x 0.1 x 0.9 / 0.01^2 = 16 x 0.09 / 0.0001 = 14,400 per group.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.stats.power <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> NormalIndPower\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.stats.proportion <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> proportion_effectsize\nes = proportion_effectsize(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.11</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.10</span>)\nn = NormalIndPower().solve_power(effect_size=es, alpha=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.05</span>, power=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.8</span>, ratio=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Levers to reduce required sample size: larger MDE, lower-variance metric, CUPED/variance reduction, one-tailed (rarely justified), accept lower power.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-where-the-rule-comes-from\">Where the rule comes from</h3>\n<p>For a two-sided test at α = 0.05 and power 80%, the required sample per group is</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.96</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.84</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>15.7</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n \\approx \\frac{2\\,(z_{1-\\alpha/2} + z_{1-\\beta})^2\\,\\sigma^2}{\\delta^2} = \\frac{2\\,(1.96 + 0.84)^2\\,\\sigma^2}{\\delta^2} \\approx \\frac{15.7\\,\\sigma^2}{\\delta^2} \\approx \\frac{16\\,\\sigma^2}{\\delta^2}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>σ²</strong> is the variance of the metric per user. For a conversion rate, σ² = p(1 − p).</li>\n<li><strong>δ</strong> is the <strong>minimum detectable effect (MDE)</strong>: the smallest <em>absolute</em> difference worth detecting.</li>\n<li>The &quot;2&quot; appears because the difference of two means has twice the variance of one mean.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-worked-examples\">Worked examples</h3>\n<p><strong>Conversion rate.</strong> Baseline 10%, you want to detect +1 percentage point (10% → 11%):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">n ≈ 16 × 0.10 × 0.90 / 0.01² = 16 × 0.09 / 0.0001 = 14,400 per group  (28,800 in total)</code></pre></div>\n<p>The exact calculation gives a close answer:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.stats.power <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> NormalIndPower\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.stats.proportion <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> proportion_effectsize\nes = proportion_effectsize(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.11</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.10</span>)                              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Cohen&#x27;s h = 0.0326</span>\nNormalIndPower().solve_power(effect_size=es, alpha=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.05</span>, power=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.8</span>, ratio=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 14,744 per group</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Continuous metric.</strong> Average order value with σ = ₹200, detect a ₹20 lift: n ≈ 16 × 200² / 20² = 1,600 per group.</p>\n<p><strong>How the MDE drives the cost</strong> (baseline 10%, per group):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>MDE (absolute)</th><th>Rule of 16</th><th>Exact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>+0.5 pp</td><td>57,600</td><td>57,756</td></tr><tr><td>+1 pp</td><td>14,400</td><td>14,744</td></tr><tr><td>+2 pp</td><td>3,600</td><td>3,835</td></tr><tr><td>+3 pp</td><td>1,600</td><td>1,768</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The rule is slightly optimistic for small baselines but excellent for mental maths. Try your own inputs:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"samplesize","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-from-sample-size-to-duration\">From sample size to duration</h3>\n<p>Suppose the page gets 6,000 eligible users a day and you split 50/50 with 100% of traffic. Needing 14,744 × 2 = 29,488 users means about 5 days, but you still run for <strong>at least 7 to 14 days</strong> to cover weekly cycles. If only 5,000 users a week are eligible, the same test would take 6 weeks: the options are a larger MDE, a lower-variance metric, or a different test design.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-levers-to-reduce-the-required-sample\">Levers to reduce the required sample</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Larger MDE</strong>: accept only bigger effects (be honest about what is worth shipping).</li>\n<li><strong>Lower-variance metric</strong>: use a more sensitive proxy (conversion rather than revenue), cap outliers, or use ratio metrics carefully.</li>\n<li><strong>Variance reduction</strong> such as <strong>CUPED</strong> (using pre-experiment behaviour; often a 30 to 50% reduction).</li>\n<li><strong>Higher traffic allocation</strong>, or more units via a longer test.</li>\n<li><strong>One-tailed test</strong> (rarely justified) or accepting lower power (with eyes open).</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout trap\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Absolute versus relative lift</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>&quot;A 10% lift&quot; on a 10% baseline can mean 11% (relative) or 20% (absolute, wildly different). The formula needs the <strong>absolute</strong> difference δ. Always say &quot;+1 percentage point&quot; or &quot;+10% relative (from 10% to 11%)&quot;.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m6-sample-size~1","q":"Your baseline conversion is 5% and you want to detect a 10% relative lift (5% to 5.5%). Estimate the sample size per group.","a":"<p>The absolute MDE is 0.5 percentage points (δ = 0.005). n ≈ 16 × p(1 − p) / δ² = 16 × 0.0475 / 0.000025 = <strong>30,400 per group</strong> (about 61,000 total). This is why low-conversion products need huge traffic to detect small relative lifts, and why teams focus on bigger changes or higher-funnel, higher-rate metrics.</p>\n","s":"16 × 0.05 × 0.95 / 0.005² ≈ 30,400 per group."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-sample-size~ca0_1","q":"Baseline conversion 10%, detect +1pp. Rough n per group?","a":"<p>16 × 0.09 / 0.01² ≈ 14,400 per group.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-sample-size~ca0_2","q":"What happens to the required sample if the minimum detectable effect is halved?","a":"<p>It quadruples, since n scales with 1/delta².</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-sample-size~ca0_3","q":"Traffic is 2,000 eligible users a day split in two groups, need 14,400 per group. Duration?","a":"<p>28,800 / 2,000 = about 14.4 days; round up to 3 full weeks.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m6-sample-size~ta1_1","q":"<p>Average order value has a standard deviation of 400 rupees. How many users per group do you need to detect a 20-rupee change, using the same rule of thumb?</p>\n","a":"<p>For a mean, the variance is σ² = 400² = 160,000. n ≈ 16 × 160,000 / 20² = 2,560,000 / 400 = 6,400 users per group. If order value is heavily skewed, the real requirement can be higher; trimming extreme values or using CUPED reduces the variance and therefore n.</p>\n"}]},"m6-pitfalls":{"id":"m6-pitfalls","slug":"a-b-testing-pitfalls","m":6,"title":"A/B testing pitfalls","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m6-lifecycle"],"tags":["ab-testing","experimentation","pitfalls"],"qa":6,"ai":{"scope":"Common ways A/B tests mislead (peeking, SRM, novelty, interference, wrong unit of analysis, seasonality, outliers) with an explanation and a fix for each.","keyTerms":["peeking","novelty effect","network effects","interference","Simpson's paradox","pseudo-replication","winsorizing","multiple comparisons"],"interviewQs":["List eight pitfalls of A/B testing.","Explain the peeking problem and one remedy.","How do you test a change in a marketplace where treatment can affect control?"],"mistakes":["Stopping a test the moment it is significant","Analysing sessions as if they were independent users","Declaring \"no effect\" from an underpowered test"],"practice":"For each pitfall in the table, write how you would detect it and how you would fix it.","related":["m6-lifecycle","m6-advanced","m5-multiple-testing"],"points":"Pitfall - Explanation / Fix\nPeeking - Checking p-values repeatedly and stopping when significant inflates false positives. Fix: fixed horizon, or sequential testing (alpha spending, mSPRT).\nSRM - Unequal split vs design signals logging/assignment bugs. Invalidate the test.\nNovelty / primacy effect - Users react to change itself; effect decays/grows. Run longer, look at new vs returning users.\nSimpson's paradox - Aggregate direction differs from segment direction due to unbalanced mix.\nMultiple comparisons - Many metrics/segments cause false positives. Pre-register, correct (BH), or label exploratory.\nNetwork effects / interference - In marketplaces/social, treatment leaks to control. Use cluster/geo randomization, switchback.\nWrong unit of analysis - Randomize by user but analyze by session inflates significance (pseudo-replication). Use delta method or user-level aggregation.\nSeasonality / day-of-week - Run full weeks.\nOutliers / heavy tails - Cap or winsorize revenue, use robust metrics or bootstrap.\nUnderpowered test - Non-significant does not mean no effect.\nSelection bias in ramp-up - Keep randomization consistent; do not let users switch variants."},"intuitive":"<p>The biggest trap in A/B testing is looking too often. If you check the results every day and stop as soon as you see a win, you will often stop on a lucky streak, like rolling dice until you get a six and declaring the dice magic. Other traps are just as sneaky: a new design is exciting at first then fades, treatment users affect control users (like drivers shared between groups), or you count visits as if they were different people. A good experimenter knows these traps and designs around them.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> &quot;What could go wrong with this experiment?&quot; is a standard follow-up question.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Fix the sample size in advance, randomise and analyse by the same unit, run full weeks, and check for SRM.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Most A/B tests fail through process, not maths. Peeking: checking repeatedly and stopping at significance. Multiple metrics or segments: some will look significant by chance. Novelty and primacy effects: users react to newness, so early lifts fade. Sample ratio mismatch: the split is not what was planned. Interference: users affect each other, as in marketplaces or social networks, so control is contaminated. Wrong unit: randomising by session but analysing by user. Underpowered tests: a non-significant result does not prove no effect. The catch: for each pitfall I know the fix, such as fixed horizons, corrections, longer runs, cluster randomisation or holdouts.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Check the split before reading the results","html":"<p>You planned a 50/50 test. After a week: 50,600 users in control and 49,400 in treatment. Close enough?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Expected counts if the split is working:</strong> 50,000 and 50,000.</li>\n<li><strong>Chi-square statistic:</strong> Σ (observed − expected)² / expected = 600² / 50,000 + 600² / 50,000 = 7.2 + 7.2 = <strong>14.4</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>p-value</strong> for chi-square 14.4 with 1 degree of freedom ≈ <strong>0.00015</strong>. A random 50/50 split would produce an imbalance this large about 1.5 times in 10,000.</li>\n<li><strong>Conclusion: sample ratio mismatch.</strong> Something is removing users unevenly: perhaps the new page crashes on some phones before the user is logged, or a bot filter treats the two arms differently.</li>\n<li><strong>Do not read the conversion numbers.</strong> The groups are no longer comparable, so any difference may come from <em>who</em> is missing rather than from the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Fix and rerun.</strong> Find where the users are lost (by device, browser or day), fix the logging or assignment, and start again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A 1.2% imbalance sounds small, but with 100,000 users it is almost impossible by chance. Check the split first; it is the cheapest bug detector in experimentation.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-watch-the-peeking-problem-happen","text":"Watch the peeking problem happen","level":3},{"id":"h-the-pitfall-table","text":"The pitfall table","level":3},{"id":"h-the-interference-problem-in-marketplaces","text":"The interference problem in marketplaces","level":3},{"id":"h-the-unit-of-analysis-error","text":"The unit of analysis error","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>Explanation / Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Peeking</strong></td><td>Checking p-values repeatedly and stopping when significant inflates false positives. Fix: fixed horizon, or sequential testing (alpha spending, mSPRT).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SRM</strong></td><td>Unequal split vs design signals logging/assignment bugs. Invalidate the test.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Novelty / primacy effect</strong></td><td>Users react to change itself; effect decays/grows. Run longer, look at new vs returning users.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Simpson&#39;s paradox</strong></td><td>Aggregate direction differs from segment direction due to unbalanced mix.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Multiple comparisons</strong></td><td>Many metrics/segments cause false positives. Pre-register, correct (BH), or label exploratory.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Network effects / interference</strong></td><td>In marketplaces/social, treatment leaks to control. Use cluster/geo randomization, switchback.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wrong unit of analysis</strong></td><td>Randomize by user but analyze by session inflates significance (pseudo-replication). Use delta method or user-level aggregation.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Seasonality / day-of-week</strong></td><td>Run full weeks.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Outliers / heavy tails</strong></td><td>Cap or winsorize revenue, use robust metrics or bootstrap.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Underpowered test</strong></td><td>Non-significant does not mean no effect.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Selection bias in ramp-up</strong></td><td>Keep randomization consistent; do not let users switch variants.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-watch-the-peeking-problem-happen\">Watch the peeking problem happen</h3>\n<p>The simulation runs A/A tests (there is no true difference) and checks for significance at several points. Even though each look uses α = 5%, stopping at the first significant look inflates the false-positive rate:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"peeking","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>In a run of 2,000 A/A tests with 10 equally spaced peeks, about <strong>21%</strong> were &quot;significant&quot; at some peek, versus the intended 5% when you look only once at the end (5 peeks gave about 15%).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-pitfall-table\">The pitfall table</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pitfall</th><th>What goes wrong</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Peeking</strong></td><td>Repeatedly checking and stopping at significance inflates false positives</td><td>Fixed horizon; or sequential methods (alpha-spending, mSPRT, always-valid p-values)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SRM</strong></td><td>Unequal split vs design signals logging or assignment bugs</td><td>Chi-square check on counts; invalidate and fix</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Novelty / primacy effect</strong></td><td>Users react to change itself; the effect decays or grows</td><td>Run longer; compare new vs returning users; look at effect over time</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Simpson&#39;s paradox</strong></td><td>Aggregate direction differs from segment direction because of unbalanced mix</td><td>Balanced randomisation; stratified analysis; check mix across arms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Multiple comparisons</strong></td><td>Many metrics and segments produce false positives</td><td>Pre-register; correct (Holm, BH); label exploratory</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Network effects / interference</strong></td><td>In marketplaces and social products, treatment spills over to control</td><td>Cluster / geo randomisation, switchback (time-based) tests</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Wrong unit of analysis</strong></td><td>Randomise by user but analyse by session inflates significance (pseudo-replication)</td><td>Analyse at the user level, or use the delta method / cluster-robust SEs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Seasonality / day of week</strong></td><td>Partial weeks skew results</td><td>Run whole weeks, avoid festivals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Outliers / heavy tails</strong></td><td>A few whales dominate revenue</td><td>Cap (winsorise), use robust or bootstrap methods</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Underpowered test</strong></td><td>&quot;Not significant&quot; is misread as &quot;no effect&quot;</td><td>Plan power; report the CI</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Selection bias in ramp-up</strong></td><td>Users switch variants; partial rollout not random</td><td>Keep assignment consistent; no cross-over</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-interference-problem-in-marketplaces\">The interference problem in marketplaces</h3>\n<p>In a two-sided marketplace (Swiggy, Uber, Flipkart), giving one group cheaper delivery shifts demand onto limited supply (riders, inventory), which <strong>changes outcomes for the control group too</strong>. The measured difference then mixes the true effect with a spillover, and often overstates the gain. Options: randomise at a coarser level (<strong>city or zone</strong>), use <strong>switchback</strong> experiments (alternate the treatment over time blocks in the same region), or model the interference explicitly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-unit-of-analysis-error\">The unit of analysis error</h3>\n<p>If 100,000 users generate 400,000 sessions and you randomise by user but run a t-test on sessions, you treat 400,000 correlated observations as independent. The standard error is too small and p-values too optimistic. Aggregate to one row per user (conversion per user, revenue per user) and then test, or use the <strong>delta method</strong> for ratio metrics.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\"># per-user analysis: one row per user, then compare the two arms</span>\nper_user = events.groupby([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;variant&quot;</span>], as_index=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>).agg(orders=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nunique&quot;</span>), revenue=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sum&quot;</span>))\nstats.ttest_ind(per_user.loc[per_user.variant == <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;B&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;revenue&quot;</span>], per_user.loc[per_user.variant == <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;A&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;revenue&quot;</span>], equal_var=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m6-pitfalls~1","q":"The experiment has run 3 days, is significant at p = 0.03, and the PM wants to ship now. What do you say?","a":"<p>Explain: (1) <strong>Peeking</strong> inflates the false-positive rate; the test&#39;s error guarantees only hold at the pre-planned sample size. (2) <strong>Three days</strong> misses weekday/weekend behaviour and possible novelty effects. (3) Early estimates of effect size are inflated (the winner&#39;s curse). Offer a path: finish the planned duration (or use a pre-agreed sequential design that allows early stopping with valid error control), and monitor guardrails meanwhile. If the decision is cheap and reversible, agree an explicit risk threshold instead.</p>\n","s":"Not yet: the planned horizon was not reached, three days cannot cover a weekly cycle, and early significance inflates false positives."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m6-pitfalls~2","q":"The test shows +2% on mobile and −2% on desktop, and the overall effect is flat. Interpret.","a":"<p>Opposite effects cancel in the aggregate. First check whether the segment results are <strong>credible</strong>: was the split pre-planned, are the sample sizes large enough for the confidence intervals to exclude zero, and does a mechanism explain it (a layout optimised for small screens)? If real, the decision is to <strong>ship to mobile only</strong> and fix or drop the desktop variant. If the segments were examined post hoc, treat it as a hypothesis and validate it with a follow-up experiment, because slicing creates spurious subgroup effects.</p>\n","s":"A genuine heterogeneous effect or noise; check power per segment, then consider shipping to mobile only."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-pitfalls~ca0_1","q":"A new design lifts clicks 8% in week one and 1% by week four. What explains it?","a":"<p>A novelty effect: curiosity inflates early engagement; run long enough to see the settled effect.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-pitfalls~ca0_2","q":"Why can a ride-sharing price test with randomised riders be biased?","a":"<p>Riders compete for the same drivers, so treatment changes supply for control (interference). Randomise by city or time block instead.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-pitfalls~ca0_3","q":"Test shows p = 0.2 with low power. Conclude no effect?","a":"<p>No: absence of evidence is not evidence of absence; report the confidence interval and the power or detectable effect.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m6-pitfalls~ta1_1","q":"<p>A new home page shows lifts in click-through of +8% in week 1, +4% in week 2, +1.5% in week 3 and +1% in week 4. What is happening, and what would you report?</p>\n","a":"<p>A novelty effect: users click more because the page is new, and it wears off. The stable effect is roughly the later weeks, about +1%. Report the week-by-week trend, base the decision on the settled effect with its confidence interval, and, if the effect matters long term, keep a small holdout group to measure it after launch. A three-day test would have reported +8%.</p>\n"}]},"m6-advanced":{"id":"m6-advanced","slug":"advanced-experimentation-ideas","m":6,"title":"Advanced experimentation ideas","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m6-pitfalls"],"tags":["ab-testing","experimentation","causal-inference"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Advanced experimentation methods with a one-line idea, when to use each, and short code: CUPED variance reduction, Bayesian A/B, bandits, and difference-in-differences.","keyTerms":["CUPED","stratification","sequential testing","Bayesian A/B testing","multi-armed bandit","difference-in-differences","propensity score","uplift modelling"],"interviewQs":["What is CUPED?","Bayesian vs frequentist A/B testing?","When would you use a bandit instead of an A/B test?","How do you estimate a treatment effect when you cannot randomise?"],"mistakes":["Using CUPED with post-treatment covariates","Treating P(B > A) as a p-value","Using bandits when you need clean inference"],"practice":"Simulate CUPED on synthetic data and report the variance reduction you get for two correlation strengths.","related":["m6-pitfalls","m6-sample-size"],"points":"- CUPED: use pre-experiment covariates to reduce variance.\n- Stratified / blocked randomization: ensure balance.\n- Sequential testing, always-valid p-values.\n- Bayesian A/B testing: report P(B > A) and expected loss.\n- Multi-armed bandits: trade off exploration vs exploitation; good for short-lived decisions (banners), less for clean inference.\n- Diff-in-diff, synthetic control, propensity score matching, instrumental variables: quasi-experimental methods when randomization is impossible (e.g., city-level policy, price change).\n- Uplift modeling: predicts incremental effect of a treatment per user."},"intuitive":"<p>These are the power tools of experimentation. CUPED is like handicapping in golf: if you know how each person did last week, you compare improvements rather than raw scores, which makes real differences easier to spot. Bayesian testing answers &quot;what is the chance B is better?&quot; directly. Bandits shift traffic toward the winner while learning, good for short-lived choices like banners. And when you cannot randomise at all (a price change in one city) you use clever comparisons like difference-in-differences.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Senior interviewers often ask &quot;how would you do this better or with less data?&quot;</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Know the name, the idea and the trade-off of each method.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Beyond the basics there are a few techniques worth naming. CUPED uses a pre-experiment covariate, like last month&#39;s spend, to reduce variance, so I can detect smaller effects with the same traffic. Sequential testing allows valid peeking by adjusting the thresholds. Bayesian A/B testing reports the probability that B beats A and the expected loss. Multi-armed bandits shift traffic towards winners to cut regret, good for short-lived choices but weaker for clean inference. Network effects need cluster or switchback designs. When randomising is impossible, I use difference-in-differences or synthetic control. The catch: each method buys something and costs something, and I should say which.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Difference-in-differences when you cannot randomise","html":"<p>A food-delivery company launches express delivery in City A only. Orders per day:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Before</th><th>After</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>City A (launched)</td><td>100</td><td>120</td><td>+20</td></tr><tr><td>City B (not launched)</td><td>90</td><td>100</td><td>+10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The naive estimate</strong> compares A before and after: +20 orders. But things change over time for everyone: season, marketing, salaries.</li>\n<li><strong>Use B to measure that background change.</strong> B rose by 10 with no launch, so +10 is the &quot;would have happened anyway&quot; trend.</li>\n<li><strong>Difference-in-differences:</strong> (120 − 100) − (100 − 90) = 20 − 10 = <strong>+10 orders a day</strong> attributed to express delivery.</li>\n<li><strong>The key assumption: parallel trends.</strong> Without the launch, A would have changed by the same amount as B. Check it by plotting both cities for several weeks <em>before</em> the launch; the lines should move together.</li>\n<li><strong>What breaks it.</strong> Something else happened only in A (a festival, or a competitor leaving), or B was affected indirectly (customers ordering across city lines).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> When you cannot randomise, find a comparison group, subtract its change, and state the assumption that makes the subtraction fair.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-cuped-variance-reduction-with-pre-experiment-dat","text":"CUPED: variance reduction with pre-experiment data","level":3},{"id":"h-other-variance-and-balance-tools","text":"Other variance and balance tools","level":3},{"id":"h-bayesian-a-b-testing","text":"Bayesian A/B testing","level":3},{"id":"h-multi-armed-bandits","text":"Multi-armed bandits","level":3},{"id":"h-quasi-experimental-methods-when-randomisation-is","text":"Quasi-experimental methods (when randomisation is impossible)","level":3},{"id":"h-uplift-modelling","text":"Uplift modelling","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>CUPED:</strong> use pre-experiment covariates to reduce variance.</li>\n<li><strong>Stratified / blocked randomization:</strong> ensure balance.</li>\n<li><strong>Sequential testing, always-valid p-values.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Bayesian A/B testing:</strong> report P(B &gt; A) and expected loss.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-armed bandits:</strong> trade off exploration vs exploitation; good for short-lived decisions (banners), less for clean inference.</li>\n<li><strong>Diff-in-diff, synthetic control, propensity score matching, instrumental variables:</strong> quasi-experimental methods when randomization is impossible (e.g., city-level policy, price change).</li>\n<li><strong>Uplift modeling:</strong> predicts incremental effect of a treatment per user.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-cuped-variance-reduction-with-pre-experiment-dat\">CUPED: variance reduction with pre-experiment data</h3>\n<p>Idea: adjust each user&#39;s metric by their own pre-experiment behaviour X (for example last month&#39;s spend), which is unaffected by the treatment:</p>\n<p><code>Y_adj = Y − θ (X − mean(X))</code>, with <code>θ = cov(X, Y) / var(X)</code>.</p>\n<p>The adjusted metric has the same expected treatment effect but variance reduced by the factor <code>(1 − ρ²)</code>, where ρ is the correlation between X and Y. With ρ = 0.7 the variance falls by about <strong>49%</strong> (you need roughly half the sample):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\nrng = np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\nx = rng.normal(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20_000</span>)                       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># pre-experiment metric</span>\ny = <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.7</span> * (x - <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>) + rng.normal(<span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">20_000</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># outcome, correlation about 0.7 with x</span>\ntheta = np.cov(x, y)[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>] / x.var(ddof=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\ny_adj = y - theta * (x - x.mean())\ny_adj.var() / y.var()                                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.508  ≈ 1 - rho^2</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Use only <strong>pre-treatment</strong> covariates (using a variable affected by the treatment biases the estimate). Users without history need special handling.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-other-variance-and-balance-tools\">Other variance and balance tools</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Stratified / blocked randomisation</strong>: randomise within strata (country, platform) so the arms are balanced on important covariates.</li>\n<li><strong>Sequential testing</strong>: methods (alpha-spending, mSPRT, always-valid p-values) that let you monitor continuously and stop early while keeping the false-positive rate valid.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-bayesian-a-b-testing\">Bayesian A/B testing</h3>\n<p>Model each arm&#39;s conversion with a Beta distribution, update with data and compare posteriors:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">a = rng.beta(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> + <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> + <span class=\"hljs-number\">900</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">200_000</span>)    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># A: 100 conversions of 1,000</span>\nb = rng.beta(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> + <span class=\"hljs-number\">120</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> + <span class=\"hljs-number\">880</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">200_000</span>)    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># B: 120 of 1,000</span>\n(b &gt; a).mean()                              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># P(B &gt; A) = 0.92</span>\nnp.maximum(a - b, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).mean()                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># expected loss if we ship B: 0.0005 (0.05 percentage points)</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>You get a direct probability and an <strong>expected loss</strong> to decide with (&quot;a 92% chance B is better, and if we are wrong we lose about 0.05 points&quot;). The frequentist z-test on the same data gives p = 0.15, a different question (how surprising is the data if there were no difference?). Bayesian methods still need discipline: priors matter for small samples, and peeking is less harmful but not free of bias in the stopping rule.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-multi-armed-bandits\">Multi-armed bandits</h3>\n<p>A bandit allocates traffic adaptively, sending more users to arms that look better, balancing <strong>exploration</strong> and <strong>exploitation</strong> (for example <strong>Thompson sampling</strong>: sample a plausible conversion rate from each arm&#39;s posterior and show the arm with the highest sample).</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">true = [<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.04</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.05</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.06</span>]; succ, fail = np.ones(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>), np.ones(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>); pulls = np.zeros(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> _ <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5000</span>):\n    k = np.argmax(rng.beta(succ, fail))                <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Thompson sampling</span>\n    r = rng.random() &lt; true[k]\n    succ[k] += r; fail[k] += <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> - r; pulls[k] += <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\npulls      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [549, 320, 4131]: most traffic ends up on the best arm</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Bandits suit <strong>short-lived</strong> decisions with low cost of exploration (banners, headlines, promotions) and reduce regret. A/B tests suit decisions needing <strong>clean, unbiased inference</strong> about a fixed effect, because adaptive allocation complicates estimation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-quasi-experimental-methods-when-randomisation-is\">Quasi-experimental methods (when randomisation is impossible)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Difference-in-differences (DiD):</strong> compare the before-to-after change in a treated group with the change in a comparable control group. Example: a fee change in Pune (treated) vs Jaipur (control). Orders per day: Pune 100 → 130 (+30), Jaipur 90 → 105 (+15). Effect ≈ 30 − 15 = <strong>+15 orders/day</strong>, assuming both cities would have trended alike (the <strong>parallel trends</strong> assumption: check it on pre-period data).</li>\n<li><strong>Synthetic control:</strong> build a weighted blend of control units that matches the treated unit&#39;s pre-period, then compare after.</li>\n<li><strong>Propensity score matching:</strong> estimate each unit&#39;s probability of treatment from covariates, match treated with similar untreated units. Only handles <em>observed</em> confounders.</li>\n<li><strong>Instrumental variables:</strong> use something that shifts treatment but affects the outcome only through it (a lottery, a distance), for hidden confounding.</li>\n<li><strong>Regression discontinuity:</strong> compare units just above and below a cutoff (a score threshold).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-uplift-modelling\">Uplift modelling</h3>\n<p>Instead of predicting who will buy, predict <strong>who will buy <em>because of</em> the treatment</strong> (the individual treatment effect), using randomised experiment data and models like a two-model approach (difference of two predictions) or meta-learners. The goal: target the <strong>persuadables</strong> and avoid &quot;sure things&quot; and &quot;do not disturb&quot; users.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m6-advanced~1","q":"You cannot run an A/B test on a new pricing policy rolled out to Mumbai only. How do you estimate its effect?","a":"<p>Choose control cities that resemble Mumbai before the change (or build a <strong>synthetic control</strong> from several), then compare Mumbai&#39;s change in the metric with the control&#39;s change (<strong>difference-in-differences</strong>). Validate by confirming the pre-period trends were parallel, check for other simultaneous changes (promotions, weather, festivals), and run a placebo test on pre-period dates or on unaffected metrics. State the limits: it relies on untestable assumptions and gives a weaker causal claim than randomisation.</p>\n","s":"Difference-in-differences against comparable cities (or a synthetic control), after checking parallel pre-trends."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-advanced~ca0_1","q":"How does CUPED reduce variance?","a":"<p>It subtracts the part of the metric predictable from pre-experiment behaviour, leaving less noise, so the same effect is detectable with fewer users.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-advanced~ca0_2","q":"When is a bandit better than a fixed A/B test?","a":"<p>When opportunity cost of a bad arm is high and the decision is short-lived, such as headline testing; for a clean causal estimate on a long-lived feature, use A/B.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-advanced~ca0_3","q":"You cannot randomise a policy change by user. What can you do?","a":"<p>A quasi-experiment: difference-in-differences with a comparable untreated group, synthetic control, or a regional rollout with matched controls.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m6-advanced~ta1_1","q":"<p>Your metric has variance 100, and the same metric measured in the month before the experiment correlates 0.8 with it. By how much can CUPED reduce the variance, and what does that mean for sample size?</p>\n","a":"<p>CUPED removes the part of the variance explained by the pre-period metric: the new variance ≈ 100 × (1 − 0.8²) = 100 × 0.36 = 36. Sample size is proportional to variance, so you need about 36% as many users for the same minimum detectable effect, nearly three times fewer. Equivalently, with the same users you can detect an effect about 0.6 times as large (√0.36 = 0.6).</p>\n"}]},"m6-cases":{"id":"m6-cases","slug":"practice-case-questions-and-self-check","m":6,"title":"Practice case questions and self-check","min":40,"type":"practice","priority":"High","prereq":["m6-pitfalls"],"tags":["ab-testing","case-study","practice"],"qa":10,"ai":{"scope":"Six practice case questions on experimentation with model answers, plus the self-check.","keyTerms":["case answers","metrics trade-off","segments","marketplace pricing","heavy tails","SRM"],"interviewQs":["The test is positive on conversion but average order value dropped. What do you do?","How do you test delivery-fee pricing on a two-sided marketplace?","What do you do when SRM is detected?"],"mistakes":["Giving an answer without a structure","Ignoring guardrails and segments","Skipping the decision rule"],"practice":"Answer each question aloud using the template (goal, metrics, unit, sizing, risks, analysis, decision), then reveal the model answer.","related":["m6-lifecycle","m6-pitfalls","m13-feature-success"],"points":"Q: You ran an A/B test for a new recommendation widget; conversion is +0.3% (p = 0.04) but average order value dropped. What do you do?\nA: \n\nQ: Test shows +2% on mobile and -2% on desktop, flat overall. Interpret.\nA: \n\nQ: How do you test a change in delivery-fee pricing on a two-sided marketplace?\nA: \n\nQ: The test has run for 3 days and is significant. Should we ship?\nA: \n\nQ: Only 5,000 users available per week; metric is revenue per user with heavy tails. Plan the test.\nA: \n\nQ: Your experiment has SRM (51.5/48.5 at n = 500k). What next?\nA: \n\nAnswer template: restate goal -> define metrics (primary, guardrail) -> unit of randomization -> sample size/duration -> risks (peeking, novelty, SRM) -> analysis plan -> decision rule.\n\n- Compute a required sample size by hand.\n- List five reasons an experiment result may be untrustworthy.\n- Explain the peeking problem and one remedy."},"intuitive":"<p>Case questions test whether you can think in a clear order under pressure. Whatever the scenario, walk through the same steps: restate the goal, choose the main measure and the &quot;do not hurt&quot; measures, decide who or what gets randomised, say how many people you need and for how long, name the risks, describe the analysis and state the rule for deciding. Practise until this order is automatic.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> This is the core of the product-company case round.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Structure beats cleverness: restate, metrics, unit, size, risks, analysis, decision.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For an experiment case I follow a script. Clarify the goal and the decision. Choose the primary metric and guardrails. Define the unit, the groups and any exclusions. Estimate sample size and duration. Name the risks: novelty, interference, seasonality. Say how I will analyse it, and what I will do for each outcome: ship, iterate or stop. For example, for a new checkout button, the primary metric is conversion to purchase, guardrails are refund rate and revenue per user, I randomise by user, run two full weeks, and ship only if the lift beats the minimum worth it. The catch: I always tie the answer back to a business decision.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A complete case answer, in order","html":"<p>Prompt: <em>&quot;We built a new ranking for restaurants on the home screen. How would you test it?&quot;</em></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify the goal.</strong> &quot;Is the aim more orders, better matches (fewer cancellations or bad ratings), or a fairer spread of orders across restaurants?&quot; Suppose: more completed orders without hurting satisfaction.</li>\n<li><strong>Metrics.</strong> Primary: completed orders per user over 14 days. Guardrails: cancellation rate, delivery time (popular restaurants may get overloaded), rating of delivered orders, and the share of orders going to new restaurants.</li>\n<li><strong>Unit and design.</strong> Randomise by user. Also note a marketplace risk: if the new ranking sends everyone to the same few kitchens, wait times rise for control users too. Watch restaurant-side load, and consider testing by city if that effect is large.</li>\n<li><strong>Size and duration.</strong> From the baseline order rate and the smallest worthwhile lift, compute n; run at least two full weeks.</li>\n<li><strong>Analysis plan.</strong> Lift with a confidence interval, guardrail checks, a sample ratio mismatch check, and segments (new versus returning users) treated as exploratory.</li>\n<li><strong>Decision rule.</strong> Ship if orders rise and the guardrails hold; if orders rise but delivery time worsens, try capping exposure for busy restaurants.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Goal, metrics, design, size, analysis, decision: the same six steps answer almost every experiment case. Naming the marketplace risk is what separates a strong answer.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-6-5","text":"6.5 Practice case questions","level":2},{"id":"c-6-6","text":"6.6 Self-check","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-answer-template","text":"The answer template","level":3},{"id":"h-self-check","text":"Self-check","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-6-5\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.5</span> Practice case questions</h2>"},{"k":"qa","id":"6.5#1","src":"c","q":"You ran an A/B test for a new recommendation widget; conversion is +0.3% (p = 0.04) but average order value dropped. What do you do?","a":"<p><strong>Restate:</strong> conversion went up (+0.3% absolute, p = 0.04) but average order value (AOV) fell, so the two metrics pull in opposite directions.</p>\n<p><strong>What I would do:</strong> (1) Evaluate the metric that combines them, <strong>revenue per visitor</strong> (conversion × AOV), with a confidence interval; a lift in conversion with a fall in AOV may net positive or negative. (2) Check <strong>why</strong> AOV fell: the widget may be recommending cheaper items, pulling users toward low-value purchases (a mix shift), or converting marginal users who buy small baskets (which is fine). Segment by new vs returning users and by category. (3) Check guardrails such as returns and margin, not just revenue. (4) Consider the evidence strength: p = 0.04 on a +0.3% effect is fragile; check power and the confidence interval, and whether the conversion gain survives a longer run.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision:</strong> If revenue per visitor (or contribution margin per visitor) is significantly positive and guardrails hold, ship; if it is flat or negative, iterate on the widget (for example recommend complementary higher-value items) and retest.</p>\n","s":"Weigh revenue, not conversion alone: +0.3% conversion with lower order value may reduce revenue per user. Check revenue/user with its interval and the guardrails before shipping.","m":"<p><strong>Numbers.</strong> If conversion rises from 10.0% to 10.03% (+0.3% relative) while order value falls 3%, revenue per visitor falls about 2.7%. Statistical significance on conversion alone hides the revenue loss.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"6.5#2","src":"c","q":"Test shows +2% on mobile and -2% on desktop, flat overall. Interpret.","a":"<p><strong>Interpretation:</strong> opposite effects in the two segments cancel to a flat overall result, so the overall average hides two real but opposite stories, if they are real.</p>\n<p><strong>Check credibility:</strong> Was the platform split pre-planned? Are the sample sizes enough for each segment&#39;s confidence interval to exclude zero? Do the pre-period metrics look balanced? Is there a mechanism (for example a layout that helps small screens and breaks large ones)? Apply a multiple-comparison correction if you looked at many segments.</p>\n<p><strong>Action:</strong> If the heterogeneity is credible, <strong>ship to mobile only</strong> and fix or roll back desktop; if it was found by slicing post hoc, treat it as a hypothesis and confirm with a follow-up test specifically on that split. Also check the mix of mobile vs desktop users between arms (Simpson-type effects).</p>\n","s":"Look for a real heterogeneous effect versus noise: check each segment&#39;s interval and whether it was pre-planned; if real, ship by platform; otherwise treat it as a hypothesis to retest."},{"k":"qa","id":"6.5#3","src":"c","q":"How do you test a change in delivery-fee pricing on a two-sided marketplace?","a":"<p>Randomising riders or customers individually is risky because the two groups <strong>share supply and demand</strong>, so treatment leaks into control (interference): a cheaper fee in treatment raises demand that competes with control users for the same riders.</p>\n<p><strong>Design:</strong> randomise at a <strong>coarser unit</strong> where spillover is contained: matched <strong>cities or zones</strong> (geo experiment), or a <strong>switchback</strong> design (alternate fee policies by time block, such as alternating days or hours within a city, and compare blocks). Choose matched pairs on historical demand, weather and festival calendars.</p>\n<p><strong>Metrics:</strong> primary: contribution margin per order or per customer (not only conversion), plus order volume, delivery time, rider utilisation, cancellations and customer retention as guardrails. <strong>Analysis:</strong> difference-in-differences or a paired comparison of zones; cluster-robust standard errors. <strong>Risks:</strong> small number of clusters (low power), carryover between time blocks, events in only some zones. <strong>Duration:</strong> long enough for behaviour to settle and to cover weekly cycles.</p>\n","s":"Two-sided interference: randomise by market or time block (switchback), measure both sides (orders, driver earnings, wait time), and watch for spillovers."},{"k":"qa","id":"6.5#4","src":"c","q":"The test has run for 3 days and is significant. Should we ship?","a":"<p><strong>No, not yet.</strong> Three days cannot cover a full weekly cycle, and <strong>peeking</strong> (stopping when the result is significant) inflates false positives: the test&#39;s error guarantees hold only at the planned sample size. Early effect sizes are also inflated (the winner&#39;s curse), and novelty effects may still be working.</p>\n<p><strong>What I would say to the PM:</strong> finish the pre-planned duration (at least one to two weeks), or use a pre-agreed <strong>sequential testing</strong> plan that permits early stopping with valid error control. Meanwhile monitor SRM and guardrails. If the change is cheap and reversible and waiting is costly, agree an explicit risk tolerance and a rollback plan.</p>\n","s":"No: three days misses weekday cycles and novelty effects, and early peeking inflates false positives; run the planned duration of whole weeks.","m":"<p><strong>Why not.</strong> Day-of-week effects, novelty and repeated peeking all inflate false positives. Decide the sample size and duration in advance and analyse once, or use a sequential method designed for early looks.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","id":"6.5#5","src":"c","q":"Only 5,000 users available per week; metric is revenue per user with heavy tails. Plan the test.","a":"<p><strong>Constraint:</strong> only 5,000 eligible users per week and a <strong>heavy-tailed</strong> metric (revenue per user) means low power and outlier-driven noise.</p>\n<p><strong>Plan:</strong> (1) Consider a <strong>more sensitive proxy</strong> as the primary metric (conversion, orders per user, or capped revenue) and keep revenue as secondary. (2) <strong>Cap (winsorise)</strong> revenue at a high percentile (for example P99) or use a log transform; or use a <strong>bootstrap</strong> for inference. (3) Use <strong>CUPED</strong> with pre-experiment revenue to cut variance substantially. (4) <strong>Compute the required sample</strong>: with high variance the MDE achievable in a few weeks may be large, so be explicit about the smallest effect we can detect in N weeks and decide whether that is acceptable. (5) Randomise by user, run a fixed horizon (for example 4 to 6 weeks), analyse per user, and monitor guardrails. If even that is underpowered, test a bolder change, or use a Bayesian/expected-loss approach for a reversible decision.</p>\n","s":"Use a variance-reduction plan: CUPED, winsorise or log-transform the heavy tail, or test a more sensitive proxy metric; compute realistic duration and accept a larger minimum effect."},{"k":"qa","id":"6.5#6","src":"c","q":"Your experiment has SRM (51.5/48.5 at n = 500k). What next?","a":"<p><strong>51.5% / 48.5% at n = 500,000 is a textbook SRM.</strong> The expected deviation under chance is about 0.07 percentage points; this is 1.5 points off (about 21 standard deviations), a chi-square p-value around 1e-100.</p>\n<p><strong>Action:</strong> treat the experiment as <strong>invalid</strong>; do not analyse or ship from it. Investigate causes: assignment bugs or caching, bot or internal-traffic filtering applied unevenly, a redirect or crash in one variant that loses users before logging, triggered-vs-assigned counting differences, different loading times causing drop-off before the assignment event. Fix the cause, verify with an <strong>A/A test</strong>, and rerun. Report the incident and add an automatic SRM alert to the experimentation platform.</p>\n","s":"Stop and debug: an SRM means assignment or logging is broken; find the cause (bots, redirects, filters) and rerun; do not interpret the results.","m":"<p><strong>What an SRM tells you.</strong> With n = 500k, a 51.5/48.5 split is about 4 standard errors from 50/50, essentially impossible by chance. Common causes: bot filtering applied unequally, a redirect that loses users in one arm, or a logging bug.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Answer template:</strong> restate goal -&gt; define metrics (primary, guardrail) -&gt; unit of randomization -&gt; sample size/duration -&gt; risks (peeking, novelty, SRM) -&gt; analysis plan -&gt; decision rule.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-6-6\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.6</span> Self-check</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m6-cases.c1","kind":"self","items":["Compute a required sample size by hand.","List five reasons an experiment result may be untrustworthy.","Explain the peeking problem and one remedy."]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-answer-template\">The answer template</h3>\n<p><strong>Restate the goal</strong> → <strong>define metrics</strong> (primary, guardrail) → <strong>unit of randomisation</strong> → <strong>sample size and duration</strong> → <strong>risks</strong> (peeking, novelty, SRM, interference) → <strong>analysis plan</strong> → <strong>decision rule</strong>.</p>\n<p>Practise each question below: think for a minute, answer aloud, then reveal the model answer and compare.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-self-check\">Self-check</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Compute a required sample size by hand (the 16σ²/δ² rule).</li>\n<li>List five reasons an experiment result may be untrustworthy (peeking, SRM, novelty, interference, wrong unit of analysis, seasonality, multiple comparisons).</li>\n<li>Explain the peeking problem and one remedy (fixed horizon or sequential testing).</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-cases~ca0_1","q":"Test a recommendation carousel on the homepage. Primary metric and guardrails?","a":"<p>Primary: click-through to purchase or revenue per user. Guardrails: page latency, bounce rate, diversity of items, returns.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-cases~ca0_2","q":"Result: conversion +0.3% with a wide interval including 0, cost to maintain is high. Decision?","a":"<p>Do not ship on this evidence; the effect is uncertain and may be below the minimum worth the cost. Extend the test or iterate.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m6-cases~ca0_3","q":"What is the first question to ask in any experiment case?","a":"<p>What decision will this result change, and what size of effect would justify it?</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m6-cases~ta2_1","q":"<p>Results come back: orders per user +1.8% (95% CI +0.6% to +3.0%), but average delivery time rose from 31 to 34 minutes and cancellations rose slightly. What do you recommend?</p>\n","a":"<p>The order lift is real (the interval is above zero), but a guardrail is clearly worse: a 3-minute increase in delivery time is likely to hurt retention later, and cancellations confirm the strain. Do not ship as is. Investigate whether the ranking concentrates orders on busy kitchens, add a load or wait-time penalty to the ranking, and re-test. Present the trade-off in numbers so the product team can decide.</p>\n"}]}});